Introduction to Programming Languages: executable companion.
Read docs/programming-languages-course.md alongside this document.
Run from the repository root:
cargo run -q -p lamlet-cli -- examples/language-theory.lam

$[Import* std]

1. Expressions, types, and evaluation.
An expression is a piece of code that produces a value.
expect: 3
${+ 1 2}

2. Surface syntax can describe the same operation in different ways.
These calls both apply neg to 3.
expect: -3
${neg 3}
expect: -3
${3.neg}

3. Functions remember the bindings visible where they were defined.
The base parameter of the caller does not replace addBase's captured base.
expect: 15
$[# base 10]
$[# {addBase x} {+ base x}]
${{^ {base} {addBase 5}} 100}

4. A lambda delays its body. The conditional macro uses this property.
expect: 7
${!if true 7 {error "the unselected branch must not execute"}}
expect: 7
${caseBool true {^ {} 7} {^ {} {error "also unselected"}}}

5. Checking a type does not change the value.
expect: 3
${: {+ 1 2} Int}

6. A let-bound function can be instantiated at different types.
expect: 7
$[# {identity x} x]
${identity 7}
expect: true
${identity true}

7. Records combine fields; variants select one labeled alternative.
expect: "Ada"
$[# person {. name "Ada" age 36}]
${.name person}
expect: "value 7"
$[# {describe option} {match option {.
        none {^ {ignored} "missing"}
        some {^ {value} [f "value $value"]}
    }}]
${describe {#some 7}}
expect: "missing"
${describe {#none unit}}

8. Tail recursion carries the remaining work as parameters.
For this example n must be nonnegative and the result must fit in Int.
expect: 55
$[# sumDown {^ loop {n acc}
    {!if {= n 0} acc {loop {- n 1} {+ acc n}}}
}]
${sumDown 10 0}

9. Immutable update returns a new value.
The original person's age is still 36.
expect: 36
$[# older {.age= person 37}]
${.age person}
expect: 37
${.age older}

10. Macros rewrite syntax; staging evaluates checked code into values.
expect: "ran"
$[Macro !unless {{condition yes no} {!if condition no yes}}]
${!unless false "ran" "skipped"}
expect: [0, 1, 4, 9, 16]
$[# squares [% {map {range 0 5} {^ {x} {* x x}}}]]
$squares

11. A closure can outlive the function call that created it.
expect: 12
$[# {makeAdder amount} {^ {x} {+ amount x}}]
$[# addFive {makeAdder 5}]
${addFive 7}

12. Arithmetic laws depend on the language's numeric semantics.
This association succeeds; (+ maximum 1) would overflow before subtracting 1.
expect: 9223372036854775807
${+ 9223372036854775807 {+ 1 -1}}
